  [1mThe fork:[0m [38;5;153mlogin(username, password)[39m is called from exactly one place — the form submit handler — and that handler only
  has the two form fields. There is no userId anywhere in this repo: no session, no stored identity, no auth response
  carrying an id ([38;5;153mlogin[39m is a stub that returns [38;5;153m{ success: true, user: username }[39m). So "add a userId parameter" needs a
  source, and the source decides whether this is a one-file edit or a new piece of the app.

  - [1mReturn the id from login instead of passing it in[0m — the server (eventually) tells you who authenticated, and
    tracking reads it off the result. No new data source, stays in [38;5;153mapp.js[39m, and the stub just grows an [38;5;153mid[39m field. Cheap to
    reverse later. Worth noting: an id you pass [3minto[0m login is circular — the caller can't know who logged in before the
    login happened. That's the reason I lean here.
  - [1mTreat [38;5;153musername[39m as the identifier[0m — cheapest of all, but then a [38;5;153muserId[39m parameter buys nothing that [38;5;153musername[39m doesn't
    already carry; this really means "no new parameter, just include it in the tracking call." Cheap to reverse.
  - [1mCaller supplies userId from something upstream[0m — a tenant id, a device/anonymous id, an existing session. This is
    the only reading that genuinely needs a new parameter, but it also needs a new source of identity in the page, and
    it changes the signature for every future caller. Expensive to undo.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m the first one. It gets you "track who logged in" without inventing an identity source, and it keeps
  [38;5;153mlogin[39m's signature honest.

  If the answer turns out to involve real tracking infrastructure (an analytics call, an audit log, anything beyond the
  existing [38;5;153mconsole.log[39m), that's a subsystem this repo doesn't have and I'll stop and re-classify this as architectural
  rather than quietly build it.
